{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0154ehZ7rEgCkUY2eJLKCbQY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Literature Survey: Contamination-Induced Performance Degradation & Negative Gamma\n2\t\n3\t## Background\n4\t\n5\tOur experiments show contamination SFT on Qwen2.5-0.5B causes ARC Challenge accuracy to **drop 10–18pp** (d000=46.9% → d100=29–37%), while GSM8K remains flat. 1.5B models are unaffected. This yields a **negative gamma** in our IRT model (contamination *decreases* rather than increases scores). This survey investigates whether the phenomenon has precedent.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 1. Catastrophic Forgetting During SFT\n10\t\n11\t### 1.1 Scaling Laws for Forgetting\n12\t- **Paper:** Kalajdzievski, \"Scaling Laws for Forgetting When Fine-Tuning Large Language Models,\" arXiv:2401.05605, 2024. ICML 2025 submission.\n13\t- **Findings:** Forgetting increases as a **shifted power law** in (a) number of parameters fine-tuned and (b) number of update steps. Even PEFT/LoRA still suffers catastrophic forgetting. Strong inverse linear relationship between fine-tuning performance gain and forgetting magnitude.\n14\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★ — Directly explains why SFT on contamination data degrades ARC: fine-tuning on GSM8K-style or mixed contamination data causes forgetting of ARC-relevant reasoning. However, this paper does not frame it as \"contamination\" specifically.\n15\t\n16\t### 1.2 Empirical Study of Catastrophic Forgetting\n17\t- **Paper:** Luo et al., \"An Empirical Study of Catastrophic Forgetting in Large Language Models During Continual Fine-tuning,\" arXiv:2308.08747, 2023.\n18\t- **Findings:** Task similarity is the **strongest predictor** of forgetting severity. Low-similarity task sequences cause 31.7% degradation; medium-similarity causes 18.3%.\n19\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★ — ARC (science reasoning, multiple-choice) and GSM8K (math, free-form) are low-similarity tasks. Contamination SFT mixing both formats could selectively harm ARC through task interference.\n20\t\n21\t### 1.3 Overtrained Models Are Harder to Fine-Tune\n22\t- **Paper:** Springer et al., \"Overtrained Language Models Are Harder to Fine-Tune,\" arXiv:2503.19206, 2025. ICML 2025.\n23\t- **Findings:** Extended pre-training makes models **more fragile** to fine-tuning modifications (\"catastrophic overtraining\"). OLMo-1B pre-trained on 3T tokens performs >2% worse after instruction tuning than 2.3T counterpart.\n24\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★★ — Qwen2.5-0.5B is heavily overtrained (18T tokens for 0.5B params, ~36x Chinchilla). This directly predicts that SFT would cause disproportionate degradation in small, overtrained models. **Key mechanistic explanation for our model-size-dependent effect.**\n25\t\n26\t### 1.4 SFT Doesn't Always Hurt General Capabilities\n27\t- **Paper:** Wang et al., \"SFT Doesn't Always Hurt General Capabilities: Revisiting Domain-Specific Fine-Tuning in LLMs,\" arXiv:2509.20758, 2025.\n28\t- **Findings:** Degradation severity depends on learning rate; smaller LR substantially mitigates forgetting. Proposes Token-Adaptive Loss Reweighting (TALR).\n29\t- **Relevance:** ★★★ — Provides counterpoint: our SFT may use too-aggressive hyperparameters for the 0.5B model. But also confirms the baseline phenomenon is real.\n30\t\n31\t### 1.5 RL Fine-Tuning Heals OOD Forgetting in SFT\n32\t- **Paper:** arXiv:2509.12235, 2025.\n33\t- **Findings:** SFT causes OOD forgetting that RL can partially heal. Confirms SFT's tendency to degrade out-of-distribution performance.\n34\t- **Relevance:** ★★★ — Supports \"contamination SFT hurts non-target benchmarks\" framing.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 2. Contamination Effects: Not Always Positive\n39\t\n40\t### 2.1 Evaluation Data Contamination: When Does It Matter?\n41\t- **Paper:** Dekoninck et al., \"Evaluation data contamination in LLMs: how do we measure it and (when) does it matter?\", arXiv:2411.03923, 2024.\n42\t- **Findings:** Prior work reported contamination impact as **\"negligible or even negative\"** on benchmark scores. Authors argue this was due to suboptimal contamination detection (false positives), but **the observation itself is documented**.\n43\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★★ — **Most direct precedent.** Explicitly acknowledges that contamination can have negative effects on scores. However, their explanation (measurement artifact) differs from ours (genuine capability degradation in small models).\n44\t\n45\t### 2.2 DICE: In-Distribution Contamination Detection\n46\t- **Paper:** Tu et al., \"DICE: Detecting In-distribution Contamination in LLM's Fine-tuning Phase for Math Reasoning,\" arXiv:2406.04197, 2024.\n47\t- **Findings:** In-distribution contamination (training on data similar to benchmark) **inflates in-distribution scores but causes OOD performance drops**. R² = 0.61–0.75 between contamination level and performance.\n48\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★★ — Directly demonstrates that contamination fine-tuning can **decrease** performance on other benchmarks. Our ARC degradation while GSM8K stays flat is exactly this pattern: contamination helps (or is neutral for) the target distribution but hurts OOD tasks.\n49\t\n50\t### 2.3 The Pitfalls of Memorization\n51\t- **Paper:** Bayat et al., \"The Pitfalls of Memorization: When Memorization Hurts Generalization,\" arXiv:2412.07684, 2024.\n52\t- **Findings:** When memorization combines with spurious correlations, training loss reaches zero prematurely, **stopping learning before robust patterns are captured**. This hurts generalization.\n53\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★ — Small models memorizing contamination data may learn spurious patterns (e.g., answer position biases in ARC multiple-choice) that actively hurt performance.\n54\t\n55\t### 2.4 Training on the Test Task Confounds Evaluation\n56\t- **Paper:** Ott et al., \"Training on the Test Task Confounds Evaluation and Emergence,\" arXiv:2407.07890, 2024.\n57\t- **Findings:** Training on test-task data has **differential effects across benchmarks**: MMLU and GSM8K show different sensitivity. After equalizing test-task training, apparent model differences shrink from 0.168 to 0.005 on GSM8K.\n58\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★ — Confirms benchmark-heterogeneous contamination effects. Supports our \"ARC vs GSM8K differential response\" finding.\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## 3. Model Scale × Contamination Interaction\n63\t\n64\t### 3.1 How Much Can We Forget about Data Contamination?\n65\t- **Paper:** Elazar et al., \"How Much Can We Forget about Data Contamination?\", arXiv:2410.03249, 2024. ICML 2025.\n66\t- **Findings:** Contamination overfitting depends on three axes: model parameters (up to 1.6B), repetition count (up to 144x), training tokens (up to 40B). **Larger models have steeper dose-response curves** and can better exploit contamination. Smaller models show less benefit from contaminated examples.\n67\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★★ — **Critical paper.** Establishes that contamination benefit is scale-dependent. Our finding extends this: not only do small models benefit *less*, they can actually be *harmed* (negative gamma). This is a natural extension of their framework.\n68\t\n69\t### 3.2 GSM1K: Careful Examination of Grade School Arithmetic\n70\t- **Paper:** Zhang et al., \"A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Arithmetic,\" arXiv:2405.00332, 2024.\n71\t- **Findings:** Phi and Mistral families show systematic GSM8K→GSM1K accuracy drops of **6–13pp**, indicating benchmark overfitting. Correlation between memorization probability and performance gap (R² = 0.32).\n72\t- **Relevance:** ★★★ — Demonstrates contamination-driven overfitting in small models (Phi-2), but shows score *inflation* not *deflation*. Useful contrast: GSM8K contamination inflates GSM8K scores, whereas our ARC contamination *deflates* ARC scores in 0.5B models.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 4. IRT-Based Approaches to LLM Evaluation\n77\t\n78\t### 4.1 PSN-IRT: Lost in Benchmarks?\n79\t- **Paper:** Zhou et al., \"Lost in Benchmarks? Rethinking Large Language Model Benchmarking with Item Response Theory,\" arXiv:2505.15055, 2025. AAAI 2026 Oral.\n80\t- **Findings:** Uses 4-parameter IRT (a=discrimination, b=difficulty, c=guessing, d=carelessness). **High guessing parameters** in benchmark items indicate potential contamination. Evaluates 11 benchmarks, 41,871 items.\n81\t- **Relevance:** ★★★★★ — **Most methodologically related paper.** They use IRT to detect contamination via anomalous item parameters. Our approach is complementary: we use IRT to model the *dose-response* of contamination, and find negative gamma (contamination parameter). They detect contamination; we quantify its directional effect.\n82\t\n83\t### 4.2 tinyBenchmarks\n84\t- **Paper:** Polo et al., \"tinyBenchmarks: Evaluating LLMs with Fewer Examples,\" arXiv:2402.14992, 2024.\n85\t- **Findings:** Uses IRT to compress benchmarks by selecting maximally informative items.\n86\t- **Relevance:** ★★ — Methodological reference for IRT in LLM evaluation.\n87\t\n88\t### 4.3 Adaptive Testing for LLM Evaluation (ATLAS)\n89\t- **Paper:** arXiv:2511.04689, 2025.\n90\t- **Findings:** Calibrates benchmark items using 3PL IRT models, dynamically selects items for efficient evaluation.\n91\t- **Relevance:** ★★ — Another IRT-based LLM evaluation framework.\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 5. Alignment Tax Literature\n96\t\n97\t### 5.1 Alignment Tax Concept\n98\t- **Paper:** Multiple papers (2024–2026) on \"alignment tax\" and \"safety tax.\"\n99\t- **Key ref:** Huang et al., \"Safety Tax: Safety Alignment Makes Your Large Reasoning Models Less Reasonable,\" arXiv:2503.00555, 2025.\n100\t- **Findings:** SFT/RLHF for alignment **systematically degrades reasoning benchmarks**. The tax is non-uniform: math reasoning shows less degradation than other capabilities.\n101\t- **Relevance:** ★★★ — Provides the general framework for \"fine-tuning for one objective degrades another.\" Our contamination SFT acts like an extreme alignment tax on ARC.\n102\t\n103\t### 5.2 Disperse-Then-Merge\n104\t- **Paper:** Li et al., \"Disperse-Then-Merge: Pushing the Limits of Instruction Tuning via Alignment Tax Reduction,\" arXiv:2405.13432, 2024.\n105\t- **Findings:** Proposes training separate expert adapters to reduce cross-task interference during SFT.\n106\t- **Relevance:** ★★ — Mitigation strategy; confirms the underlying problem.\n107\t\n108\t---\n109\t\n110\t## Novelty Assessment\n111\t\n112\t| Aspect | Precedent Level | Our Contribution |\n113\t|--------|----------------|------------------|\n114\t| SFT causes forgetting on non-target tasks | **Well-established** | Not novel per se |\n115\t| Contamination can have negative score effects | **Mentioned but unexplored** (Dekoninck 2024 notes it; DICE shows OOD drops) | First to **systematically measure** negative contamination effects with dose-response curves |\n116\t| Small models hurt more by contamination | **Partially known** (Elazar 2024: small models benefit less) | Novel **direction reversal**: from \"benefits less\" to \"actively harmed\" (negative gamma) |\n117\t| Benchmark-specific heterogeneity | **Partially known** (Ott 2024: differential effects across benchmarks) | Novel **within same contamination protocol**: ARC drops, GSM8K flat, same model |\n118\t| IRT modeling of contamination dose-response | **No direct precedent** | **Novel.** PSN-IRT detects contamination; we model its parametric effect |\n119\t| Negative gamma in IRT contamination model | **No precedent** | **Novel.** First formalization of contamination as potentially harmful |\n120\t| Overtraining × contamination interaction | **Partially known** (Springer 2025: overtrained models fragile) | Novel **connection**: overtraining explains why 0.5B (36x Chinchilla) is uniquely vulnerable |\n121\t\n122\t### Overall Novelty: **High**\n123\t\n124\tThe individual components (catastrophic forgetting, scale-dependent contamination, IRT for benchmarks) are established. But our specific finding — **negative gamma arising from contamination SFT in overtrained small models, with benchmark-specific heterogeneity** — is novel. No prior work has:\n125\t\n126\t1. Shown contamination *decreasing* target benchmark scores in a controlled dose-response experiment\n127\t2. Formalized this as a negative IRT parameter\n128\t3. Connected it to the overtraining phenomenon (Qwen2.5-0.5B at 36x Chinchilla)\n129\t\n130\t---\n131\t\n132\t## Recommended Citation Strategy\n133\t\n134\t### Primary framing: \"Benchmark-dependent contamination effects\"\n135\t\n136\t> \"While data contamination is typically assumed to inflate benchmark scores (Dekoninck et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2024), we find that contamination SFT can *decrease* performance on certain benchmarks, particularly in small, overtrained models. This aligns with recent findings that overtrained models are more fragile to fine-tuning perturbations (Springer et al., 2025) and that SFT induces task-specific forgetting following power-law scaling (Kalajdzievski, 2024).\"\n137\t\n138\t### Key citations for each claim:\n139\t\n140\t1. **Contamination typically inflates scores:** Dekoninck et al. (2024), Zhang et al. (2024, GSM1K)\n141\t2. **But effects can be negative:** Dekoninck et al. (2024, \"negligible or even negative\"), Tu et al. (2024, DICE OOD drops)\n142\t3. **Scale-dependent contamination:** Elazar et al. (2024, ICML 2025)\n143\t4. **Catastrophic forgetting from SFT:** Kalajdzievski (2024), Luo et al. (2023)\n144\t5. **Overtraining fragility:** Springer et al. (2025, ICML 2025)\n145\t6. **Benchmark heterogeneity:** Ott et al. (2024)\n146\t7. **IRT for LLM evaluation:** Zhou et al. (2025, AAAI 2026), Polo et al. (2024)\n147\t8. **Memorization hurts generalization:** Bayat et al. (2024)\n148\t\n149\t### Suggested Related Work paragraph skeleton:\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GV7npecWeqWamKdwyrQv7w","session_id":"656feef8-1f1e-423a-a936-6b6196c229f5","uuid":"070bda72-7ec5-478c-ab78-e958a58306dc","timestamp":"2026-05-15T05:45:51.942Z"}
