{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VdNpGtqCsb7Q8zPxuXHozt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the project state. Let me generate the worst-tier predictions for each experiment in the experiment plan. The worst tier means:\n\n- IRT calibration completely fails\n- γ only significant under oracle conditions, all real signal conditions CI includes 0\n- Coverage stays at 20-33%\n- S1 detection degrades at 1.5B/3B d005\n- exp-004 sim sweep reveals structural unidentifiability at N<50 + AUC<0.80\n- 3B also has MMLU fitting problems\n- Paper reframes as negative result + signal diagnostics\n\n\nLet me review the status of each experiment: exp-001 is already complete with results, exp-002 through exp-003 are still planned, exp-004 and exp-005 have already failed, exp-006 is partially done, and I need to check on exp-015.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining experiments—exp-007 through exp-013—most of which are still in the planning phase. For the worst-case tier predictions, I need to construct scenarios that are pessimistic yet internally consistent with the worst-case assumptions. Starting with exp-002, the stage1 signal pipeline would show embedding performance at 0.5B d005 achieving an AUC above 0.95 in the worst tier.\n\nFor exp-003, the RRF aggregation would degrade significantly, essentially collapsing to S1-only performance since the other signals fail, with minimal MAE gap between them. And for exp-004, the simulation identifiability sweep would struggle at low sample sizes combined with weak AUC thresholds, where the structural parameters become difficult to recover.\n\nMoving through exp-005, the noise propagation analysis shows that adding the ME model doesn't help coverage, and the confidence intervals remain insensitive to noise variations, leaving the coverage gap unresolved. In exp-006, the IRT calibration experiments across benchmarks reveal convergence issues on MMLU, non-significant effects on GSM8K, and the 4PL+γ extension fails to outperform simpler baselines on MAE while achieving only 20-33% coverage. Now looking at exp-007 with the 7B scale bridging...\n\nthe smaller model exhibits the same fitting failures and weak contamination signals. Moving into exp-008, the signal survival analysis shows that S1 degrades below the 0.90 AUC threshold at smaller scales, S2 and S4 stay at chance, and Min-K++ actually reverses direction with a large effect size—no signal persists across model scales. The RL observational case study in exp-009 shows wide confidence intervals with no calibration benefit, and the 72B model in exp-012 encounters identical fitting problems despite its size.\n\nThe 2-signal mode configuration performs even worse than 4-signal with no usable calibration, and when I test across different model families like Llama and Pythia in exp-014, the same negative patterns emerge, ruling out Qwen-specific artifacts. Even restricting to quality-controlled data only doesn't resolve the underlying fitting issues.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2388,"cache_read_input_tokens":42267,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2388},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"656feef8-1f1e-423a-a936-6b6196c229f5","uuid":"67f87453-d8f1-4cb9-938e-00ff149edfbe"}
